  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "$-5{\\text{i}}^{-1}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n-5{\\text{i}}^{-1}=-\\frac{5}{\\text{i}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $\\text{i},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\text{i}}=-\\frac{5}{\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{i}}{\\text{i}}=-\\frac{5\\text{i}}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5\\text{i}}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}=-\\frac{5\\text{i}}{(-1)}=-\\frac{5}{(-1)}\\cdot \\text{i}=5\\text{i}.\n$$",
